# Custom PHP MVC Rules & Coding Standards

You are assisting a developer in a custom, lightweight, modern PHP MVC boilerplate called **Spartan**. 

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **CRITICAL RULE FOR AI CODE GENERATION:**
> Never code based on generic framework assumptions (such as Laravel, Symfony, or Yii defaults). You MUST strictly adhere to the custom Spartan API.
> - **Zero-Dependency Core (Optional vendor/)**: The core framework is designed to run 100% on native PHP with a custom built-in autoloader. However, external composer packages are fully supported and can be utilized or recommended if a feature or test specifically requires specialized external libraries.
> - **No Generic Helpers**: Do NOT use helper functions like `view()`, `request()`, `response()`, `redirect()`, `session()`, `env()`, or `config()` unless they are explicitly defined in Spartan's core.
> - **No Direct Globals**: Never use `$_GET`, `$_POST`, `$_FILES`, `$_SESSION`, `$_REQUEST`, or `$_SERVER` directly inside controllers, models, or services. Use the injected `Request` and `Session` abstractions (e.g. `$request->file('avatar')` to retrieve uploaded files).
> - **No Native Redirects/Headers**: Never call `header("Location: ...")` or `http_response_code()`. Always route redirects and status codes through the `Response` object.
> - **No Implicit ORM Magic**: Never assume implicit relationship mappings or auto-resolved table names. Always explicitly define table names, foreign keys, and relationship aliases.
> - **No Auto-installed Packages**: Do not import or assume third-party library namespaces (e.g. Carbon for dates, Illuminate components) unless explicitly instructed. Use native PHP objects.

Follow these specifications and structural rules when writing, refactoring, or generating code.

## 1. Project Directory Structure

```
├── config/
│   └── config.php          # Main config loader (loads .env parameters)
├── public/
│   ├── .htaccess           # URL rewrites to index.php
│   └── index.php           # Front Controller — loads route files only
├── routes/                 # Route definitions split by domain
│   ├── web.php             # Public-facing pages
│   ├── admin.php           # Protected dashboard routes
│   └── api.php             # JSON API endpoints
├── src/
│   ├── Core/               # Core framework layers (Do not modify unless requested)
│   │   ├── Application.php      # App Orchestrator
│   │   ├── Cache.php            # Cache facade (file/redis driver)
│   │   ├── CacheDriverInterface.php
│   │   ├── CacheDrivers/
│   │   │   ├── FileCacheDriver.php   # Default — zero dependencies
│   │   │   └── RedisCacheDriver.php  # Optional — requires phpredis
│   │   ├── Container.php        # Service Container (DI)
│   │   ├── Controller.php       # Base Controller
│   │   ├── Database.php         # PDO connection singleton
│   │   ├── EventDispatcher.php  # Synchronous event system
│   │   ├── Middleware.php       # Route middleware abstraction
│   │   ├── Model.php            # Base Model
│   │   ├── QueryBuilder.php     # Fluent SQL query builder
│   │   ├── Request.php          # HTTP Request handler
│   │   ├── Response.php         # HTTP Response handler
│   │   ├── Router.php           # Router (regex matcher)
│   │   ├── Session.php          # Session & Flash messages
│   │   ├── Validator.php        # Input validation rules engine
│   │   └── View.php             # View compiler
│   ├── Controllers/        # App Controllers (Extend App\Core\Controller)
│   ├── Events/             # Event name constants
│   ├── Listeners/          # Event listener classes (must implement handle())
│   ├── Middlewares/        # Route Middlewares (Extend App\Core\Middleware)
│   ├── Models/             # App Models (Extend App\Core\Model)
│   ├── Services/           # Business logic services (no extends required)
│   └── Views/              # View templates
│       ├── layouts/        # Layout wrappers (main_blade.blade.php, main.php)
│       ├── [view].blade.php # Blade template (Preferred)
│       └── [view].php      # Native PHP template (Fallback)
├── storage/
│   └── cache/              # File cache storage (auto-created)
├── .env                    # Environment variables
├── composer.json           # Composer PSR-4 namespaces
└── README.md               # Setup and development guide
```

---

## 2. Coding Conventions & Clean Architecture

- **Strict Types**: Always write `declare(strict_types=1);` at the top of every PHP file.
- **Namespacing**: App namespace is `App\`. Files under `src/` must match their folders:
  - `src/Controllers/` -> `namespace App\Controllers;`
  - `src/Models/`      -> `namespace App\Models;`
  - `src/Core/`        -> `namespace App\Core;`
  - `src/Middlewares/` -> `namespace App\Middlewares;`
  - `src/Services/`    -> `namespace App\Services;`
  - `src/Listeners/`   -> `namespace App\Listeners;`
  - `src/Events/`      -> `namespace App\Events;`
- **Strict Separation of Concerns**:
  - **No SQL outside Models**: Absolutely NO database queries, direct PDO calls, or SQL strings are allowed in Controller or View files. All data access must be encapsulated inside Model files extending `App\Core\Model`.
  - **No Output escaping inside Core/Request**: Inputs are read raw to prevent encoding corruption. Output escaping is done exclusively in Views.
- **Security Rules (all mandatory)**:
  - **XSS**: Every variable echoed inside a View or Layout MUST be escaped. Use `{{ $var }}` or `@escape($var)` in Blade templates, and `$this->escape($var)` in native PHP templates. Raw `echo $var` or `<?= $var ?>` without escaping is forbidden.
  - **CSRF**: Every HTML `<form method="POST">` MUST contain CSRF protection. Use `@csrf` in Blade templates, or `<?= $this->csrfToken() ?>` in native PHP templates. The Router validates all POST requests automatically across form, AJAX, and JSON bodies.
  - **Session Fixation**: `$this->session->regenerate()` MUST be called immediately after every successful login or role/privilege change. Never skip this.
  - **Session Cookie**: Session is automatically started with `HttpOnly=true`, `SameSite=Lax`, and `Secure` (auto-detected). Never call `session_start()` manually outside of `Session::__construct()`.
  - **View Path Safety**: Never pass user-controlled input directly to `$this->render()`. View names are whitelisted to `[a-zA-Z0-9_/]` only. Path traversal attempts (`../../`) throw an `InvalidArgumentException`.
  - **Application Singleton**: `Application::$app` is `readonly` (PHP 8.1+). Never attempt to reassign it. The constructor enforces single instantiation with a `LogicException`.
  - **Open Redirect**: Never pass user-supplied input directly to `$this->redirect()`. Always validate or whitelist the target URL first.
  - **Debug Mode**: Never hardcode `display_errors = 1`. Error visibility is controlled exclusively by the `APP_DEBUG` key in `.env`.
  - **DB Testing**: Use `Database::swapInstance($mockPdo)` to inject a test double in test suites. Use `Database::reset()` between tests.
  - **Validation**: Never write raw `isset()` / `empty()` / `filter_var()` validation chains inside Controllers. Always use `$this->validate($data, $rules)`.

---

## 3. Core Class Usage Guidelines

### Controllers
- Create controllers under `src/Controllers/` and extend `App\Core\Controller`.
- The base constructor automatically injects `$this->request`, `$this->response`, and `$this->session` — use these properties directly instead of accessing the global `Application::$app` singleton.
- Action methods should return `string` (rendered view content) or void (when redirecting/returning JSON).
- Use `$this->redirect('/path')` and `$this->json($data)` shortcuts instead of calling Response directly.
- Render views using `$this->render('view_name', ['param' => $value])`.
- For HTMX / AJAX updates, use the shortcut `$this->renderViewOnly('view_name', ['param' => $value])` to compile and return the partial template content without layout wrapping.

### Request & File Uploads
- Access all inputs and files through the injected `$this->request` instance.
- **Input parameters:** Use `$this->request->get($key)`, `$this->request->post($key)`, `$this->request->input($key)`, or `$this->request->getBody()`.
- **File Uploads:** Never access `$_FILES` directly. Use:
  - `$this->request->file('field_name')` to retrieve a specific file metadata array.
  - `$this->request->getFiles()` to get all uploaded files.
- **Upload Path:** Do not hardcode paths. Retrieve the target directory from application configuration: `Application::$app->config['storage']['uploads']`.

### Models
- Create models under `src/Models/` and extend `App\Core\Model`.
- Always declare the table property: `protected string $table = 'my_table';`.
- **NEVER write raw SQL strings inside model methods.** Use the QueryBuilder instead.
- Access the QueryBuilder via `$this->table()` (uses model's own table) or `$this->table('other_table')`.

#### QueryBuilder API Reference
```php
// SELECT
$this->table()->get();                                      // all rows
$this->table()->where('active', 1)->get();                  // filtered
$this->table()->where('role', 'admin')->first();            // first match or null
$this->table()->find($id);                                  // by primary key
$this->table()->where('status', 'paid')->count();           // count
$this->table()->where('active', 1)->exists();              // boolean

// Chaining
$this->table()
     ->select('id', 'name', 'email')                       // specific columns
     ->where('active', 1)
     ->where('role', ['admin', 'manager'], 'IN')           // IN clause
     ->orderBy('name', 'DESC')
     ->limit(10)
     ->offset(20)
     ->get();

// GROUP BY + HAVING
$this->table('orders')
     ->select('status')
     ->groupBy('status')
     ->having('cnt', 5, '>=')
     ->get();
// Note: use a raw alias in select for aggregate — HAVING column must match

// Pagination — returns ['data'=>[], 'total'=>int, 'per_page'=>int, 'current_page'=>int, 'last_page'=>int]
$page   = (int) $this->request->get('page', 1);
$result = $this->table()->where('active', 1)->paginate(15, $page);
// $result['data']         => rows for this page
// $result['last_page']    => total pages

// INSERT — returns last inserted ID
$id = $this->table()->insert(['name' => 'Ali', 'email' => 'ali@x.com']);

// UPDATE — returns affected row count
$count = $this->table()->where('id', $id)->update(['status' => 'active']);

// DELETE — returns deleted row count
$count = $this->table()->where('id', $id)->delete();

// Cross-table (advanced)
$orders = $this->table('orders')->where('user_id', $userId)->get();

// JOIN — for multi-table queries (supports both 3-arg legacy and 4-arg with operator)
$this->table('orders')
     ->join('users',    'orders.user_id',   'users.id')         // 3-argument (operator defaults to '=')
     ->leftJoin('coupons', 'orders.coupon_id', '=', 'coupons.id') // 4-argument (with custom operator)
     ->select('orders.id', 'users.name', 'coupons.code')
     ->where('orders.status', 'paid')
     ->get();
```
- Database transactions: `$this->beginTransaction()`, `$this->commit()`, `$this->rollBack()`.
- Or use the transactional wrapper which auto-commits/rolls back on failure:
  ```php
  $this->transaction(function ($model) {
      $model->table('orders')->insert([...]);
      $model->table('invoices')->insert([...]);
  });
  ```

> **Safety rules for write operations:**
> - `delete()` — **requires** at least one `where()` condition. Throws `LogicException` if called without one.
> - `update()` — **requires** at least one `where()` condition. Throws `LogicException` if called without one.
> - `truncate()` — use explicitly when you intentionally want to delete ALL rows from a table.
>
> ```php
> // ✅ correct — conditional delete
> $this->table()->where('id', $id)->delete();
>
> // ✅ correct — intentional full-table clear
> $this->table('temp_jobs')->truncate();
>
> // ❌ throws LogicException
> $this->table()->delete();
> ```

### Routes
- **Never define routes directly in `public/index.php`.** Use the route files in `routes/`.
- `routes/web.php`   — public pages (no auth required)
- `routes/admin.php` — protected pages (attach AuthMiddleware)
- `routes/api.php`   — JSON endpoints (attach TokenMiddleware if stateless)
- Register new route files in `public/index.php` with `require_once $routesPath . '/new_domain.php';`
- **Supported HTTP methods**: `get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`.
- **Form method spoofing**: HTML forms can only send GET/POST. To use PUT/PATCH/DELETE from a form, add a hidden field:
  ```html
  <form method="POST" action="/items/5">
      <?= $this->csrfToken() ?>
      <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="DELETE">
      ...
  </form>
  ```
  The Router detects `_method` on POST requests and routes to the correct handler. CSRF is still enforced (real transport is POST).
- **CSRF Exclusions**: To exclude specific stateless routes (like payment webhooks or API endpoints) from central CSRF checks, register them in route files using `$app->router->excludeCsrf()`:
  ```php
  $app->router->excludeCsrf(
      '/api/stripe/webhook',
      '/api/v1/*'
  );
  ```

### Middlewares
- Create middlewares under `src/Middlewares/` extending `App\Core\Middleware`.
- Register in the route file:
  ```php
  use App\Middlewares\AuthMiddleware;
  use App\Middlewares\SecurityHeadersMiddleware;
  $app->router->get('/admin', [AdminController::class, 'dashboard'], [
      SecurityHeadersMiddleware::class,
      AuthMiddleware::class,
  ]);
  ```
- **`SecurityHeadersMiddleware`** — sets `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, `Referrer-Policy`, `X-XSS-Protection`, and `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'`. Attach to any route that serves HTML pages.
  - To allow external assets (CDN fonts, scripts), extend the CSP by constructing the middleware manually:
    ```php
    // In a route file, not via class-name string — pass the instance directly
    // Note: middleware array accepts class names (zero-arg) or pre-built instances.
    new SecurityHeadersMiddleware("default-src 'self'; font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com")
    ```
- See `src/Middlewares/AuthMiddleware.php` as a working example.
- **Middleware Groups**: Register custom middleware groups globally to avoid repetitive declarations on individual routes:
  ```php
  $app->router->middlewareGroup('web', [
      SecurityHeadersMiddleware::class,
  ]);
  
  $app->router->middlewareGroup('admin_auth', [
      SecurityHeadersMiddleware::class,
      AuthMiddleware::class,
  ]);

  // Use the group name in route declarations:
  $app->router->get('/dashboard', [DashboardController::class, 'index'], ['admin_auth']);
  ```

### Request & Response
- Retrieve request parameters using the typed properties injected in the base Controller:
  - `$this->request->get('key')` — query string ($_GET)
  - `$this->request->post('key')` — request body ($_POST)
  - `$this->request->input('key')` — POST then GET fallback
  - `$this->request->getBody()` — all inputs as array (POST wins over GET for duplicate keys)
- To redirect: `$this->redirect('/path')` (Controller shortcut).
- To return JSON: `$this->json(['status' => 'ok'])` (Controller shortcut).
- Direct `Application::$app->request` access is reserved for use OUTSIDE Controllers (e.g. Middlewares, CLI scripts).

### Validation
- Use `$this->validate($data, $rules)` inside any Controller action that receives user input.
- Supported rules (pipe-separated): `required`, `string`, `integer`, `email`, `min:N`, `max:N`, `confirmed`, `in:a,b,c`, `nullable`, `regex:/pattern/`, `unique:table,column`.
- Check result with `$v->fails()` and retrieve errors with `$v->errors()` (all fields) or `$v->error('field')` (single field).
- **`nullable`** — if the field is absent or empty, all subsequent rules for that field are skipped. Place it first in the rule string:
  ```php
  'phone' => 'nullable|string|min:7'
  ```
- **`regex:/pattern/`** — value must match the PCRE pattern (include delimiters):
  ```php
  'slug' => 'required|regex:/^[a-z0-9-]+$/'
  ```
- **`unique:table,column`** — verifies no existing row has this value. Column defaults to the field name if omitted:
  ```php
  'email' => 'required|email|unique:users,email'
  'username' => 'required|string|unique:users'   // column = username
  ```
- Example:
  ```php
  $v = $this->validate($this->request->getBody(), [
      'email'    => 'required|email|unique:users,email',
      'password' => 'required|min:8|max:64',
      'phone'    => 'nullable|string|min:7',
  ]);
  if ($v->fails()) {
      return $this->render('register', ['errors' => $v->errors()]);
  }
  ```

### Session & Flash Messages
- Set secure session values: `$this->session->set('key', $value)`.
- Retrieve session values: `$this->session->get('key')`.
- **After login or any privilege change**: ALWAYS call `$this->session->regenerate()` to prevent session fixation.
- Create a flash message for the next request cycle: `$this->session->setFlash('status', 'Saved successfully!')`.
- Display flash messages in Views using `$this->flash('status')`.

### Service Container
- Register services in `routes/web.php` or `public/index.php` after `$app` is instantiated:
  ```php
  Application::$app->container->singleton(MailService::class, fn() => new SmtpMailer(config('mail')));
  Application::$app->container->bind(PdfExporter::class, fn() => new PdfExporter());
  ```
- Resolve inside a Controller using the shortcut: `$mailer = $this->make(MailService::class);`
- Services live in `src/Services/` and must NOT extend Model or Controller.
- Use Container for any class that has external dependencies or must be swappable in tests.
- **Controller Dependency Injection (DI)**: Controllers resolved by the router automatically resolve constructor parameters using reflection and container bindings. If a controller constructor has dependencies, type-hint them and the container will automatically instantiate/resolve them:
  ```php
  namespace App\Controllers;

  use App\Services\PaymentService;

  class OrderController extends Controller {
      public function __construct(private PaymentService $payment) {
          parent::__construct();
      }
  }
  ```
  If a controller has no constructor, it instantiates instantly (`new $controller()`) with zero reflection overhead, ensuring peak execution speed.

### Cache
- **Rule**: Never query the database for data that rarely changes. Use `Cache::remember()` instead.
- Default driver is `file` (zero dependencies). Switch to `redis` via `CACHE_DRIVER=redis` in `.env`.
  ```php
  use App\Core\Cache;

  // Fetch or store (recommended pattern)
  $products = Cache::remember('active_products', 3600, fn() => $this->table()->where('active', 1)->get());

  // Manual control
  Cache::put('key', $value, 600);
  $val = Cache::get('key', 'default');
  Cache::forget('key');
  Cache::flush(); // clear all
  ```

### Models — Timestamps
- Set `protected bool $timestamps = true;` on any model that has `created_at` / `updated_at` columns.
- Use `$this->create($data)` instead of `$this->table()->insert($data)` — timestamps are auto-injected.
- Use `$this->save($id, $data)` instead of `$this->table()->where('id', $id)->update($data)` — `updated_at` is auto-injected.
- You may still override timestamps manually by passing them explicitly in `$data`.
  ```php
  class Post extends Model
  {
      protected string $table     = 'posts';
      protected bool   $timestamps = true;

      public function publish(int $id): int
      {
          return $this->save($id, ['status' => 'published']); // updated_at auto-set
      }

      public function createDraft(array $data): string|false
      {
          return $this->create($data); // created_at + updated_at auto-set
      }
  }
  ```

### Model Relationships

Lightweight relationship system — explicit, no magic, no lazy loading.

**Define relationships in the Model:**
```php
use App\Core\RelationQuery;
use App\Models\Order;
use App\Models\Profile;
use App\Models\User;

class User extends Model
{
    protected string $table = 'users';

    // One user has many orders (foreign key on orders table)
    public function orders(): RelationQuery
    {
        return $this->hasMany(Order::class, foreignKey: 'user_id');
    }

    // One user has one profile (foreign key on profiles table)
    public function profile(): RelationQuery
    {
        return $this->hasOne(Profile::class, foreignKey: 'user_id');
    }
}

class Order extends Model
{
    protected string $table = 'orders';

    // Each order belongs to one user (foreign key on THIS table)
    public function user(): RelationQuery
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(User::class, foreignKey: 'user_id');
    }
}
```

**Single record:**
```php
$user    = (new User)->find(1);
$orders  = (new User)->orders()->for($user);    // array of order rows
$profile = (new User)->profile()->for($user);   // single row or null

$order = (new Order)->find(5);
$user  = (new Order)->user()->for($order);      // single user row or null
```

**Collection — eager loading (NEVER use for() inside a loop):**
```php
// ❌ N+1 — 1 query per user:
foreach ($users as $user) {
    $user['orders'] = (new User)->orders()->for($user); // new query each iteration
}

// ✅ Eager load — 2 queries total regardless of collection size:
$users = (new User)->all();                                       // query 1
$users = (new User)->orders()->loadFor($users, as: 'orders');    // query 2
// $users[0]['orders'] → [order, order, ...]
// $users[1]['orders'] → [order, ...]

// belongsTo eager load:
$orders = (new Order)->all();                                     // query 1
$orders = (new Order)->user()->loadFor($orders, as: 'user');     // query 2
// $orders[0]['user'] → user row or null
```

- **`foreignKey` is always required** — never derived automatically. Be explicit.
- **`loadFor()` as key defaults**: `hasMany` → class name + 's' (e.g. `'orders'`), `hasOne`/`belongsTo` → class name (e.g. `'user'`). Always pass `as:` explicitly for clarity.
- Relationships return plain arrays — consistent with the rest of the framework.

### Events & Listeners
- **Rule**: Any side effect after a primary action (SMS, email, inventory, log) must be a Listener — never inline code inside a Controller.

- Dispatch from a Controller shortcut: `$this->event('order.placed', $orderArray);`
- Register listeners in `routes/web.php` or a dedicated boot file:
  ```php
  use App\Listeners\SendOrderSms;
  use App\Listeners\UpdateInventory;

  // Sync — executes immediately (fast DB updates, logging)
  Application::$app->events->listen('order.placed', UpdateInventory::class);

  // Async — pushed to jobs table, worker executes later (SMS, email, HTTP calls)
  Application::$app->events->listen('order.placed', SendOrderSms::class,
      async: true, maxAttempts: 3, onFailure: 'retry'
  );
  Application::$app->events->listen('order.placed', SendEmail::class,
      async: true, maxAttempts: 1, onFailure: 'stop'
  );
  ```
- Listener classes must implement `public function handle(mixed $payload): void`.
- See `src/Listeners/OrderPlacedListener.php` as a working example.

### Async Jobs & Queue
- **Rule**: Any listener that calls an external API, sends SMS/Email, or generates a file MUST be registered with `async: true`.
- **Prerequisites**: Run `storage/jobs.sql` once to create the `jobs` table.
- **Worker**: Process pending jobs via CLI:
  ```bash
  # Single pass — run once and exit (for Cron, every minute)
  php worker.php

  # Loop mode — poll every 5 seconds (for development / daemon)
  php worker.php --loop

  # Cron example:
  # * * * * * php /var/www/project/worker.php >> /var/log/worker.log 2>&1
  ```
- **Failure behaviour**:
  - `onFailure: 'retry'` — retries with exponential backoff: attempt 2 → +1 min, attempt 3+ → +5 min.
  - `onFailure: 'stop'`  — marks failed immediately on first error. Use for non-idempotent operations.
- **Fallback guarantee**: If the DB is unavailable when dispatching, async listeners fall back to sync execution automatically (logged to error_log). The request never silently drops events.
- **`maxAttempts`** minimum is 1. Setting it to 1 with `onFailure: 'retry'` is equivalent to `onFailure: 'stop'`.
- **Callable listeners are always sync** — async requires a class name string.

### Middleware & Rate Limiting
- **Rule**: Middlewares inspect and filter incoming HTTP requests. They can abort request resolution early (e.g. on redirects, authorization failure, or rate limiting) to bypass executing controller callbacks.
- **Alias Mapping**: Register aliases in the Router constructor or dynamically via `$router->aliasMiddleware('name', MiddlewareClass::class)`.
- **Parameterized Middlewares**: Specify parameters after a colon: e.g. `'rate_limit:60,60'`. Parameters are passed directly to the constructor of the middleware.
- **Rate Limit Middleware (`rate_limit:limit,window`)**:
  - Automatically mapped to `App\Middlewares\RateLimitMiddleware`.
  - Tracks requests per client IP (`Request::getIp()`) and route path.
  - Returns `429 Too Many Requests` when limit is exceeded and sets headers: `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, `X-RateLimit-Reset`, and `Retry-After`.
  - Uses the Cache layer (`Cache::get` and `Cache::put`).
  ```php
  // Register in routes/web.php or routes/api.php
  $router->get('/api/resource', [ApiController::class, 'index'], ['rate_limit:100,60']);
  ```

### Global Exception Handler
- **Rule**: All application errors and exceptions are routed through the global `App\Core\ExceptionHandler` to prevent raw server leakage.
- **Customization**: To swap or customize how errors are rendered (e.g. returning JSON for API routes vs error page templates), bind your custom class in the container:
  ```php
  use App\Core\ExceptionHandler;
  
  Application::$app->container->singleton(ExceptionHandler::class, fn() => new MyCustomExceptionHandler());
  ```

---

## 4. Workflow to Add a New Feature

When the developer requests a new feature (e.g. "Add a blog detail page"), follow these steps:
1. **Define Route**: Register in the appropriate `routes/` file (`web.php`, `admin.php`, or `api.php`). Include Middlewares as the third argument.
2. **Register Services/Listeners** (if needed): Add Container bindings and Event listeners in the route file or a boot file.
3. **Create/Update Model**: If database integration is required, create the model in `src/Models/`. Use `$this->table()->...` — never raw SQL.
4. **Create Service** (if needed): For complex multi-step logic, create a `src/Services/MyService.php`. Register it in the Container.
5. **Create Controller Action**: Fetch inputs via `$this->request`, resolve services via `$this->make()`, query Models, dispatch events via `$this->event()`, return rendered view or JSON.
6. **Create View Template**: Create `.blade.php` in `src/Views/` to use the built-in native Blade compiler (supports `@csrf`, `@flash`, `@escape`, and `{{ $var }}` auto-escaping), or `.php` to write native PHP templates (requires manual `$this->escape()` and `<?= $this->csrfToken() ?>`).
7. **Cache where applicable**: Wrap expensive database reads in `Cache::remember()` with a sensible TTL.

---

## 5. Views & Frontend Standards (V)

- **Native Blade Engine**: The Blade rendering engine is implemented natively inside the core (`src/Core/View.php`), extracted from the spirit of the previous BladeOne library to maintain complete syntax compatibility. It uses regex compilation and recursive PCRE patterns to support layout inheritance, section buffering, loops, conditional evaluations, and partial includes without requiring any external dependencies (like eftec/bladeone).
- **Preferred Template Format**: Always prioritize using Blade templates (`.blade.php`) over native PHP templates (`.php`) for any new user interfaces or pages. Native PHP templates are maintained strictly as fallback.
- **Strict Layout Inheritance**: All Blade views that serve complete pages MUST extend a base layout using `@extends('layouts.main_blade')` (or equivalent) and define content blocks inside `@section('content')` and `@endsection`.
- **Zero Raw Echoes**: Never use raw echoes (`<?= $var ?>`, `echo`, or `{!! $var !!}`) inside templates unless the variable is specifically marked as safe HTML. For everything else:
  - In Blade, use double curly braces `{{ $var }}` (which automatically runs HTML escaping via the built-in compiler) or `@escape($var)`.
  - In native PHP templates, use `$this->escape($var)`.
- **Form Safety**: Every single HTML `<form>` that makes a POST request MUST include the CSRF protection directive:
  - `@csrf` in Blade templates.
  - `<?= $this->csrfToken() ?>` in native PHP templates.
- **HTMX Partial Swaps**: Any route/controller action triggered by HTMX (e.g. `hx-get`, `hx-post`) that expects a targeted swap inside a page container MUST return ONLY the partial HTML code.
  - The controller MUST return the view using `$this->renderViewOnly('template_name', $data)`.
  - Never return a full layout page for HTMX swaps, as it duplicates headers/scripts and breaks the DOM.
- **Alpine.js for Client State**: Keep JavaScript in templates minimal. Use Alpine.js (`x-data`, `x-init`, `x-model`, `x-on`) for interactive client-side components (like toggle menus, counters, notifications, and client calculations). Avoid inline raw jQuery or heavy vanilla `<script>` tags.

